explanation.md:
Before loops, repetition meant copy-paste.

```c
printf("5 x 1 = 5\n");
printf("5 x 2 = 10\n");
printf("5 x 3 = 15\n");
/* ... 100 more lines */
```

That is not a style complaint, it is a correctness problem. Change the number, change the message, or print to 1000 rows, and you have to edit every copy — and you will miss one. A loop replaces *N copies of the statement* with *one statement plus a counter*.

The entire job of a loop is to answer one question: **how many times, and when do I stop?** Everything else in this topic is detail.

```c
for ( init ; test ; step ) { body }
```

The execution order is the part that gets memorised wrong. It is:

1. `init` runs **once**, before anything else.
2. `test` is evaluated. **If false, the loop exits immediately** — the body never runs.
3. `body` runs.
4. `step` runs.
5. Go back to step 2.

Two consequences people miss:

- **A `for` loop can run zero times.** `for (i = 10; i < 5; i++)` never executes its body. If a program must do something at least once, `for` is the wrong tool — that is what `do...while` is for.
- **The `step` runs *after* the body.** This matters the moment you write `continue`:

```c
for (i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;
    printf("%d ", i);
}
/* prints: 1 2 4 5  — NOT 1 2 3 4 5, and NOT an infinite loop */
```

`continue` jumps to the **step**, not back to the test. That is why the loop still terminates.

If you understand this, you never have to memorise the `for` syntax again.

```c
for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { body }
```

is exactly equivalent to

```c
i = 0;
while (i < n) { body; i++; }
```

So `for` is not a different mechanism. It is `while` with the counter's lifecycle written next to the condition so you cannot forget to update it. That is the whole reason `for` exists as a separate keyword — it is a *safety* feature, not a new capability.

```c
for (i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
    if (i > 10 && i % 2 == 0) continue;  /* skip this one */
    if (i > 20) break;                    /* leave the loop entirely */
    printf("%d ", i);
}
```

- `break` leaves the **innermost** loop (or `switch`) immediately.
- `continue` skips the rest of the current iteration.

**Nested loops:** `break` only breaks the inner loop. If you need to leave both, that is a signal your loop nesting is wrong, or that you need a flag:

```c
int found = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n && !found; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
        if (a[i][j] == target) { found = 1; break; }
    }
}
```

Prefer restructuring over flags when you can. A flag variable is a loop with two exit conditions wearing a disguise.

A nested loop runs the inner loop once for **every** iteration of the outer one. That is `O(n × m)`.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
        count++;   /* 1,000,000 iterations */
```

One million iterations is fine. One billion is not, and on a phone it is already too slow at ten million. The rule that matters:

> **Before you write a second loop inside the first, ask what you can precompute.**

The classic example is summing all pairs of an array. `O(n²)` is the naive answer; hoisting the inner sum into a running total makes it `O(n)`:

```c
/* O(n^2) — recomputes sum(a[0..j]) every time */
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
        total += sum(a, i, j);

/* O(n) — keep a running prefix sum */
long long prefix = 0, total = 0;
for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
    total += prefix * a[j];
    prefix += a[j];
}
```

You do not need the clever version on day one. You need to **know it exists**, so that when `n` grows you recognise the shape.

This bug costs beginners more hours than any other in C, so it gets its own section.

`scanf("%d", &n)` **leaves the newline in the input buffer**. On the next `scanf`, that newline is still there, and `%d` skips whitespace — including newlines — so it waits for a real number. The symptom: the program appears to ignore the first input.

```c
int age, height;
scanf("%d", &age);
scanf("%d", &height);   /* may appear to skip */
```

**The fix:** read a line of text and convert it with `strtol`, which also gives you error handling for free.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char line[100];
long v = strtol(line, NULL, 10);   /* v is 0 and errno is set if it failed */
```

**Inside a loop, this becomes input validation** — a real, idiomatic use of `while`:

```c
int n;
do {
    printf("Enter a number: ");
    scanf(" %d", &n);      /* note the leading space — skips whitespace */
} while (n < 0);
```

That leading space in `" %d"` is the idiom: **a space in the format string means "skip any whitespace, including newlines"**. It is the smallest fix and it is worth memorising.

A cleaner habit that avoids the whole class of bug: read with `fgets` into a buffer and parse with `strtol`/`strtod`. Slower to write, and it never surprises you.

